::GEO_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A figure is a straight path with arrows on both ends. Which description is most precise? {
=A line #Correct. A line is a straight path that extends forever in both directions, and arrows on both ends show that extension.
~A segment #A segment has two endpoints. Arrows on both ends show the figure does not stop at endpoints.
~A ray #A ray has one endpoint and extends in one direction. This figure extends in both directions.
~An angle #An angle is formed by two rays with a common endpoint. The description gives one straight path.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A figure has endpoints A and B. Which description matches the definition? {
~Line AB #A line extends forever in both directions and does not have endpoints.
~Ray AB #A ray has one endpoint and extends in one direction, so two endpoints do not match the definition.
=Segment AB #Correct. A segment is a part of a line with two endpoints, so endpoints A and B identify segment AB.
~Angle AB #An angle is formed by two rays with a common endpoint. Two letters alone do not name a standard angle here.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement is most precise? {
~There is a corner at B. #This is informal and does not name the rays or the angle.
=Rays BA and BC form angle ABC. #Correct. This names the two rays and the angle they form.
~The shape bends at B. #This is vague and does not use precise Geometry language.
~B is somewhere in the figure. #This does not describe the geometric relationship clearly.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student says a figure is a ray because it looks short. What is the best correction? {
~A ray is always the shortest object in a diagram. #Length in a drawing does not define a ray. A ray is defined by one endpoint and extension in one direction.
~A ray must have two endpoints. #This confuses a ray with a segment. A ray has one endpoint.
~Any short figure is a segment. #Appearance is not enough evidence. The defining features are endpoints and arrows.
=Use endpoints and arrows, not appearance, to identify the object. #Correct. Definitions and markings provide evidence; appearance alone is not proof.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which phrase best describes evidence in a Geometry explanation? {
~A guess based on what the diagram looks like #A guess is not evidence. Geometry explanations need support from definitions, labels, markings, or given information.
=Information that supports a conclusion #Correct. Evidence supports a conclusion using definitions, labels, markings, or given statements.
~A word that sounds mathematical #Mathematical vocabulary helps only when it is used accurately and supported by meaning.
~A conclusion without a reason #A conclusion needs evidence. A statement without a reason does not explain the Geometry.
}
